A||||8||general||Handling on wet pavement||||5||
Z||000000||general||05-31-2000||08:48 PM||||    I have spoken to several fiero owners who have experienced their cars wanting to go straight even though the road was making a turn in wet weather.Is this something characteristic of the fiero due to the engine weight in the rear(specifically a V-6)or maybe due to it's light weight?Doe's anyone out there have any suggestions to help fiero's to stick to the road better in wet conditions or do we just need to stick a block of wood under the accelerator pedal during rainy weather so that they won't literally fly ????    general||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||06-01-2000||01:48 AM||||Understeer, Fiero's are made that way, the slicker it is the more it shows.<P>A stiffer rear swaybar helps for a moderate amount of work and money.<P>I'm guessing you don't want to spend your entire life's savings on suspension mods.<BR>If I'm wrong you do wanna spend, just say the word, the forum has several members who actually race their Fiero's and can point you towards the correct mods for exceptionally sticky driving.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Ed||06-01-2000||01:51 AM||||The 1984 original equipment Goodyear Eagles were pretty bad in the rain, but that was years ago.<P>Stock tires are pretty low profile which may result in hydroplaning. Many cars have even lower profile tires which may cause real squirreliness in the rain.  <P>An extreme fix would be to go to narrow tires in the winter: better in snow too.     ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||06-01-2000||01:58 AM||||I cant remember what tires i had on the car.  But it was raining outside earlier, and the temperature was very cold.  I was going into the parking lot, to get to pepboys.  Its one of those outside malls with walmart, target, etc...   I was in the lot and taking a tight 90 degree right turn, and i probably was going to fast and spun my car in a complete circle.  I was lucky this was at night and no one was around.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||06-01-2000||01:05 PM||||Fieros have very little weight on front wheels to start with. In wet all tires hydroplane=lift off the road on a cushion<BR>of water. The narrower the tire, the less hydroplaning. Also use a pattern that allows the water to escape. ie@ ones with deep straight tread pattern or like Goodyear with groves at angle to push it out the sides. Finally, when its wet-slow down.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||06-01-2000||02:45 PM||||<BR>Three nights ago while I was sitting in the front room, I heard a couple of loud thumps coming from outside. I looked out just in time to see some ricer totally out of control doing donuts up the <B><I>SIDEWALK</I></B>!!! This guy was (originally) going in a straight line on a <B>wet</B> road, somehow lost control, jumped the curb, sheared off a 20 year old boulevard tree, knocked over a brick fence, took out a stop sign, and eventually came to rest on an embankment in a neighbor's yard. The car was thrashed. He must have travelled about 200 feet in this manner. If anyone had been walking on the sidewalk they would have been dead. You've got to respect wet roads, no matter what you're driving or what tires you have on.||||reg||4
Z||000006||Oreif||06-01-2000||02:55 PM||||I have never liked Goodyear tires because of there poor wet weather abilities. I prefer BFGoodrich. I also have driven on Michelins and Dunlop's Both of these are decent for adverse weather. The biggest thing about driving in rain or snow is use common sense. <BR>As stated above slow down. In the case of the ricer all I have to say is "Stupidity breeds Excitement" Do something really stupid and excitement is sure to follow. I saw a kid in a 77 malibu racing down a wet street. The car went into a slide and he hit a very healthy oak tree. Needless to say he sheared the front of the car off at the firewall and then attempted to tell the police he was only doing 25mph!||||reg||1
Z||000007||gixxer||06-01-2000||06:50 PM||||Last Sunday I made a little tour around Mt. St. Helens in my 88 Formula in the rain. The car has brand new Goodyear GT+4s in the front , and year old +4s in the back. Stock sizes. I felt very comfortable, the car had a general tendancy of mild understeer with oversteer available if I booted it in the lower gears. It only plowed the front end once, when I braked to hard on turn entry going into a hairpin(I almost dropped my bike there once too).<BR>A lot of people don't realise that for tires to stick well in the rain they have to be "fresh". Just having adequate tread depth doesn't guarantee that the tire will work well. A new tire is the best in the rain. After a couple of years the rubber hardens up, because various oils and solvents "gas" out of the rubber, and the tire looses traction. Technically speaking, the tire finishes it's curing process while you drive.<BR>I'm thinking of this only because my lo miles 88 had the ORIGINAL front tires on it when I bought it. And even though they had plenty of rubber left, they were hard as a rock! They were bad handling in the dry, they would have been dangerous in the wet.<BR>Oh yeah, I don't think there's anything inherent about a mid engine design that makes them worse in the wet. IMO<BR>Sorry for the length. <BR>Gixxer ||||reg||3
Z||000008||Wannabe||06-01-2000||11:23 PM||||The condition of the road surface makes a huge difference in grip in the wet. Many people have been surprised as they drove around a curve where the road surface changes. There are spots you can go screaming around a corner or nail the brakes without sliding, and then others where you can barely tocuh the brakes without locking up.||||reg||1
